Non-reserving version IDs can race object commits
Link: https://chatgpt.com/codex/cloud/security/findings/542ccfa2f8b48191bbc8f2c60bc125db?repo=https%3A%2F%2Fgithub.com%2Fjustincormack%2Fargmin&sev=
Criticality: medium (attack path: medium)
Status: fixed

# Resolution
Fixed in commit `a22ce27` (`Route object version allocation through metadata commands`).

The fix makes object version allocation command-owned. `next_version_id` remains
a read-only candidate helper inside a PG, but versioned publish paths no longer
consume that candidate directly. Instead they first publish a
`ReserveObjectVersion` metadata command on the object PG command stream, then
use the reserved `VersionId` in the later object publish/delete-marker command.

This closes the reported race because concurrent versioned writers for the same
bucket/key now serialize through the pending metadata-command stream before a
version ID is consumed. The retry path also reuses matching pending
`ReserveObjectVersion` commands after partial replica apply, so transient apply
failures converge instead of generating a duplicate candidate.

The direct PUT path now performs fallible conditional/action validation before
reserving an object version, so rejected writes do not advance
`object_version_counters`. Stream PUT finalization, multipart completion, and
lifecycle current expiry all reserve their version IDs through the same command
stream.

Regression coverage added in the fix:
- partial-apply retry for `ReserveObjectVersion`;
- direct PUT action failure does not reserve or advance an object version;
- versioned direct PUT and multipart completion allocate ordered versions via
  the command stream;
- versioned stream PUT finalization reserves through the command stream;
- lifecycle current expiry reserves the delete-marker version through the
  command stream.

Verification before the fix commit:
- `cargo nextest run -p storage`
- `cargo clippy --all-targets --all-features -- -D warnings`
- `cargo nextest run`
- `git diff --check`

