# Summary
Introduced a race by changing next_version_id from a reserving allocator into a read-only candidate allocator without ensuring all versioned object commit paths publish a pending command before choosing the candidate version ID.
Before this commit, next_version_id reserved a version by updating object_version_counters in an immediate SQLite transaction. The new implementation only reads the maximum existing object version and stored counter, and the counter is advanced later during metadata command application. Some commit paths compute the candidate version ID before registering their metadata command as pending. In particular, direct PUT computes the version in prepare_commit_direct_put_object_command and only later sets the pending command after payload ack registration, while multipart completion computes its version and only much later attempts to set the pending command. Since direct PUT and multipart completion use different bucket-scoped locks, two concurrent commits to the same versioned key can both observe the same read-only candidate. If one command applies and removes the pending entry before the other registers its command, the stale command may proceed with a duplicate version_id and fail during object insertion due the objects primary key. This is primarily an availability/integrity-of-operation issue: an authenticated writer, or an anonymous writer to a public-write bucket, can race commits to make concurrent writes fail after payload work has already been performed. The primary key prevents silent overwrite, limiting the impact.

# Validation
## Rubric
- [x] Confirm `next_version_id` no longer reserves/advances `object_version_counters` before returning.
- [x] Confirm at least one real commit path computes a version before publishing its pending metadata command.
- [x] Confirm direct PUT and multipart completion can interleave because they do not share the same bucket-scoped serialization lock.
- [x] Dynamically demonstrate two pre-publication readers can obtain the same non-null version id for the same bucket/key.
- [x] Dynamically demonstrate that after one command applies, a stale duplicate-version command fails on the `objects(bucket,key,version_id)` primary key, causing a write-operation failure rather than silent overwrite.
## Report
Validated the suspected race/failure mode with a targeted debug unit-test PoC plus LLDB trace. Code review showed `PgMetadataStore::next_version_id` is now a candidate reader: it queries `MAX(version_id)` from `objects` and `next_version_id` from `object_version_counters`, but performs no reserving write before returning (`crates/storage/src/pg_store.rs:7909-7982`; trait comment also says "candidate version_id ... without reserving it" at `crates/storage/src/traits.rs:291-298`). The durable counter is advanced later inside object metadata application (`crates/storage/src/pg_store.rs:5792-5825`). The direct PUT path prepares the command and chooses the version before pending command publication (`crates/storage/src/cluster.rs:1848-1855`), and then registers payload shard acks before `set_pending_metadata_command_for_bucket` (`crates/storage/src/cluster.rs:1705-1730`). Multipart completion uses a separate multipart-completion bucket lock (`crates/storage/src/cluster/request_ops.rs:4916-4928`), chooses `next_version_id` at `request_ops.rs:4988-4990`, and only later publishes pending at `request_ops.rs:5138-5144`. I added `poc_non_reserving_next_version_id_allows_duplicate_stale_commit_failure`, which models two commit paths reading the candidate before either is applied/published. The test output proves both reads return version 1, the first commit advances the next candidate to 2, and the stale second command fails at object insertion with SQLite primary-key/unique-constraint failure: `UNIQUE constraint failed: objects.bucket, objects.key, objects.version_id`. I attempted crash-style validation with a debug cargo test; there is no process crash because the vulnerability manifests as an application-level write failure. Valgrind was unavailable in the container; ASan was attempted but stable Rust rejected `-Z sanitizer=address`. LLDB was available and stopped immediately after the stale `apply_metadata_command(&second).unwrap_err()` returned, confirming the failure path under a debugger.

