## Narrative
The finding is real and in scope. The production HTTP server dispatches PutObject and CompleteMultipartUpload to coordinator/storage commit paths. In the introduced code, next_version_id only reads existing object rows and object_version_counters and does not reserve the returned value; the durable counter is advanced later during metadata command application. Direct PUT computes its version before publishing a pending metadata command and only publishes after payload shard ack registration. Multipart completion computes its version under a separate multipart-completion bucket lock and also publishes pending later. Therefore, a writer who can race direct PUT and multipart completion for the same versioned key can cause both paths to select the same version_id. The SQLite primary key prevents silent overwrite, so the proven impact is a failed stale commit/write availability degradation rather than data disclosure, auth bypass, or persistent corruption. This supports medium severity, not high or critical.
## Controls
- SigV4 authentication for non-anonymous requests
- Bucket/object authorization and ACL checks
- Public access block can disable effective public-write ACLs
- Bucket versioning gate; non-versioned writes use VersionId::Null
- SQLite primary key on objects(bucket,key,version_id) prevents silent overwrite
- HTTP max_connections and max_inflight_requests backpressure
- HTTP header/body idle timeouts
## Blindspots
- Static review did not run an end-to-end HTTP race against a live argmin-s3 process in this stage.
- No deployment manifests or load balancer configuration were present, so public internet exposure cannot be confirmed from repository artifacts.
- Exact exploit reliability depends on runtime timing, object size, storage latency, and whether operators enable bucket versioning and public-write ACLs.
- The validation PoC was storage-level rather than a complete remote exploit, although the repository code maps the affected paths to normal HTTP S3 operations.
